 dhwty-writes asked: 
 Hello mindset, I know I ask stuff frequently, it's probably the dumbest and I hope that I don't annoy you (or that you can understand what I mean bc English isn't my 1st language). And this question isn't so much about meta but about something else: I'm reading asoiaf in English and Asha sometimes calls her uncles uncle and sometimes nuncle. Why does she do that? What's the difference? And is this normal in English or just grrm-style? Because in my language they appear to mean exactly the same. 
 "uncle" and "nuncle" mean the same thing. Though "nuncle" is not used in English these days; GRRM took it from archaic dialectal English where that word was not uncommon. (Other GRRM-style words from archaic English include "pease" for peas, "neeps" for turnips, and so on.)
 "nuncle" in particular comes from the phrase "mine uncle", rebracketed into "my nuncle". That's also where we get the nickname "Ned" for Edward or Edmund (or Eddard or Edric), from "mine Ed" rebracketed into "my Ned". It's also how "Nell" became a nickname for Eleanor or Ellen, and "Nan" for Ann. Linguistics is neat. :)
