 gemfyre asked: 
 That hanged vs hung thing you posted got me wondering. Why is the word "raper" used in the books/WesterosEssos instead of "rapist" as is common English?
 I think GRRM's doing it to sound more archaic and "fantasy medieval English", as ‐er is a Middle English/Germanic suffix whereas ‐ist is French/Latin. And "raper" fits well with "murderer", "robber", etc.
 However, it's still a fake. :) Both "raper" and "rapist" are fairly modern words - per my OED they date from the US, late 1800s. "Raper" is an even newer word, first recorded 1878, vs 1869 for "rapist".
 (Maybe I should stop complaining about the use of "okay" in ASOIAF fanfics... nah.)
 If GRRM had wanted to use a truly medieval word, he should have gone with "ravisher", which dates back to the 14th century to mean "rapist". (And even appears in Shakespeare with that meaning.) But "ravish" doesn't have the same connotation these days, so I get why not.
